-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Building ATHENA
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA is built with CMake. The old GNU autotools build files are not maintained
for ATHENA.

The supported and tested development path is Linux. The build system still
contains inherited Windows and macOS branches, but the ATHENA build process has
not been tested on Windows or macOS.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dependencies
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA intentionally requires a modern desktop development environment. The
CMake configuration currently requires, among others:

  CMake, a C/C++ compiler, pkg-config, Qt 5, Guile, mimalloc, SQLite3, Python 3,
  uv, FreeType, PNG, JPEG, zlib, zstd, ImageMagick/MagickWand, Iconv, GnuTLS,
  GMP, Ghostscript, resvg with ResvgQt.h, and libtcc.

On openSUSE, this is a useful zypper baseline:

  sudo zypper install \
    git cmake ninja pkgconf-pkg-config \
    libqt5-qtbase-devel libqt5-qtsvg-devel \
    guile-devel mimalloc-devel sqlite3-devel \
    python3 python313-uv \
    freetype2-devel libpng16-devel libjpeg8-devel zlib-devel \
    libzstd-devel ImageMagick-devel \
    libgnutls-devel gmp-devel ghostscript resvg-devel

Package names vary by distribution and repository. If CMake reports that
ResvgQt.h or libtcc is missing, install the corresponding development package
from your distribution or a local package/source build. ATHENA uses libtcc for
namespace sorter support.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Recommended compiler: Intel oneAPI ICPX
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA strongly recommends Intel oneAPI C++ (icpx). The project is large, and
icpx has been the preferred compiler during current ATHENA development.

Example oneAPI setup on an RPM/zypper based system:

  sudo rpm --import \
    https://yum.repos.intel.com/oneapi/GPG-PUB-KEY-INTEL-SW-PRODUCTS.PUB
  sudo zypper addrepo -f https://yum.repos.intel.com/oneapi oneAPI
  sudo zypper refresh
  sudo zypper install intel-oneapi-compiler-dpcpp-cpp

Before configuring ATHENA, load the oneAPI environment:

  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  which icx
  which icpx

If the build directory was previously configured with another compiler, remove
it before switching compilers:

  rm -rf build

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Configure and build
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

From the repository root:

  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo
  CCACHE_DISABLE=1 cmake --build build -j "$(nproc)"

Using all available processor cores is recommended; `nproc` is the preferred
way to pick the parallelism level.

If you are not using Ninja, omit `-G Ninja` and use your chosen CMake generator.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Run the build
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For ATHENA development, do not run the executable directly from `build/src`.
Copy it into the runtime tree first:

  cp -f build/src/ATHENA.bin ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin

Then run:

  ./ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin

This matters because ATHENA expects its Scheme code, packages, tools, assets,
and runtime paths to be arranged under the ATHENA tree.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Install
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For a system install, use the usual CMake install workflow:

  cmake --install build

Set a custom prefix at configure time if needed:

  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/athena

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Troubleshooting
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If CMake cannot find a dependency, install the corresponding development
package and rerun the configure step. If generated files or Scheme cache entries
interfere with a local development build, remove the build directory and ATHENA
cache, then configure again:

  rm -rf build
  rm -rf ~/.ATHENA/system/cache
  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo

